---
import Service from '../../layouts/Service.astro';

const faq = [
  { q: 'When should rehabilitation start after surgery?', a: 'Your surgeon sets the timeline. For many procedures (knee replacement, ACL reconstruction) rehab starts within days of discharge. Book before your operation if possible to secure appointments.' },
  { q: 'What should I bring to the first session?', a: 'Your physiotherapy prescription, the operative report, any rehabilitation protocol from your surgeon, recent X-rays, and clothing that gives easy access to the operated area.' },
  { q: 'Is the practice accessible on crutches?', a: 'Yes, the practice is on the ground floor, about 3 minutes’ walk from Ranelagh metro.' },
];
---
<Service
  lang="en"
  title="Post-Surgery Rehabilitation Paris 16 – ACL, Shoulder, Knee Replacement"
  description="English-speaking physiotherapist in Paris 16th for rehabilitation after orthopaedic surgery: ACL reconstruction, knee or hip replacement, rotator cuff repair, fractures."
  eyebrow="Post-surgery rehabilitation · Paris 16th"
  h1="Post-surgery rehabilitation in Paris 16th"
  lead="Knee, shoulder, hip or ankle: rigorous rehabilitation after orthopaedic surgery, following your surgeon’s protocol, by a physiotherapist with hospital orthopaedic experience."
  crumb="Post-surgery rehabilitation"
  faq={faq}
>
  <h2>Hospital orthopaedic experience</h2>
  <p>From 2023 to 2025, Anastasia Mischenko worked in the orthopaedic department of Ambroise-Paré Hospital (AP-HP) in Boulogne-Billancourt. This experience with surgical patients gives her a solid knowledge of surgeons’ protocols, the precautions specific to each procedure and the warning signs to look out for.</p>

  <h2>Procedures covered</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Knee:</strong> ACL reconstruction, total or partial knee replacement, meniscectomy, meniscal repair;</li>
    <li><strong>Shoulder:</strong> rotator cuff repair, Latarjet or Bankart stabilisation, shoulder replacement;</li>
    <li><strong>Hip:</strong> total hip replacement;</li>
    <li><strong>Ankle and foot:</strong> Achilles tendon repair, ligament reconstruction;</li>
    <li><strong>Fractures</strong>, operated or not (wrist, ankle, tibial plateau…).</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Stages of recovery</h2>
  <p><strong>Protection:</strong> pain and swelling control, gradual recovery of range of motion, muscle activation, strictly within your surgeon’s instructions.</p>
  <p><strong>Recovery:</strong> strengthening, balance and gait work, regaining everyday movements.</p>
  <p><strong>Return to activity:</strong> more dynamic exercise, strength and control testing, preparation for work, sport and leisure.</p>
</Service>
